# Typed Pharmacological Evidence and Connectome-Dependence Testing: A Computational Method for Drug Simulation on a Synapse-Resolution *Drosophila* Connectome

**Research article, submitted draft for the International Journal of Research in Computing (IJRC)**

Software: https://github.com/pinkysworld/FlyLab
Version {{flylab_version}}, commit `{{git_sha}}`, compound library SHA-256 `{{library_sha256}}`, map `{{map_id}}`.

> Every numeric span in this document is generated by `scripts/reproduce_paper.py` from `papers/results.json`; the prose is written around the numbers, not the other way round. No number in this paper was measured in a living animal.

## Abstract

Connectome simulation and receptor pharmacology are both computational practices with good tooling, but a drug prediction made on a wiring diagram has no way to say which of its inputs it actually depends on. We present FlyLab, a method and an implementation that couples heterogeneous published pharmacological evidence to compound- and concentration-specific perturbation of named cells in the public MaleCNS v1.0 adult *Drosophila* central nervous system, and then subjects the resulting predictions to four tests the coupling itself makes possible. The method contributes: a **type system for pharmacological evidence**, in which the parameter a source measured and the distance of that source from the modelled target jointly decide which transformation is permitted, so unsupported receptors return N/A instead of a plausible small number; **connectome-dependence analysis**, which places every prediction on an information ladder of degraded graphs and reports the weakest graph model that already reproduces it; an **ablation ladder** that asks which layer of the pipeline carries the ordering information; and a **conclusion-stability matrix** that re-derives every qualitative claim under a prespecified family of admissible engagement-to-gain transformations. Evaluated over a {{ev_compounds}}-compound library, the method separates predictions that need the specific wiring — exactly the chloride-channel blockers — from a majority that are reproduced by any graph with the same transmitter composition, and shows that the paper's own headline suppression result is a property of one modelling choice rather than of the integration. A variance-based uncertainty budget identifies the engagement-to-gain transformation as the dominant assumption and converts it into a ranked experiment list. The whole analysis regenerates identically from committed artifacts, natively and in a browser. The central limitation is unchanged and structural: FlyLab evaluates the behaviour of a model, and its gain rules are asserted rather than measured.

**Index terms:** computational method, connectomics, *Drosophila melanogaster*, in-silico pharmacology, evidence typing, null models, sensitivity analysis, specification robustness, reproducible research, 3Rs.

## 1. Introduction

A public synapse-resolution connectome invites a question it cannot answer on its own: *at the concentration at which a compound engages its insect target and not its vertebrate counterpart, what happens to a named cell?* Answering it requires joining two literatures with incompatible units. Pharmacology supplies potency parameters measured in heterologous preparations, often in another species, and frequently absent altogether. Connectomics supplies a netlist with a predicted transmitter per cell and no ligand, no concentration and no receptor subtype.

Joining them is not, by itself, a contribution. Executable circuits built from fly connectome data already exist as a platform [68]; leaky integrate-and-fire models over the whole adult brain recover sensorimotor controls without fitting [7]; and on the human side, whole-brain models are routinely perturbed through receptor-density maps to simulate drug action [69], [70]. What is missing in all of them is a way to answer, for each prediction, *which of its inputs it depends on* — and a discipline that prevents missing evidence from becoming a number. That is what this paper contributes.

We organise the work around four research questions.

* **RQ1.** Can heterogeneous published pharmacological evidence be turned into computational perturbations *without* erasing its provenance or its parameter semantics?
* **RQ2.** Which pharmacological effects require the specific MaleCNS topology, and which are reproduced by coarse network statistics?
* **RQ3.** Which conclusions survive alternative admissible specifications of the model, and which assumptions dominate its uncertainty?
* **RQ4.** Can the entire analysis be reproduced identically, natively and in a browser, from auditable artifacts?

The answers are, in order: yes, by typing the evidence and refusing invalid transformations; a minority of predictions, and they are a chemically coherent minority; not all of them — the paper's most quotable circuit result is specification-dependent, and one modelling choice carries most of the variance; and yes.

### 1.1 Related work and what is new here

**Virtual animals without neurons.** Teaching simulators such as RatCVS [1], its browser successors [2] and immersive variants [3], [4] implement organ-level dose–response. They are parameterised pharmacologically and evaluated educationally [4], but contain no named cell, so no circuit question can be asked of them.

**Connectome runtimes without pharmacology.** FlyWire [5] and MaleCNS v1.0 [6] publish netlists with per-cell transmitter predictions [10], [12]. Shiu *et al.* [7] show a literature-parameterised LIF model over such a netlist reproduces sugar-driven motor output and its bitter veto; downstream ports repeat the exercise with varying rigour [11]. FlyBrainLab [68] goes further, providing an interactive platform in which executable circuits are constructed directly from fly brain data and compared — but a compound, if present, enters as a hand-set multiplier with no receptor and no concentration behind it.

**Receptor-informed whole-brain models.** On human structural connectomes, pharmacological intervention is simulated by scaling regional synaptic responses with PET receptor-density maps [69], [70]. This is the closest published relative of our approach, and its most relevant result for us is a negative one: in Mindlin *et al.* [69] the improvement produced by a simulated intervention correlated with the *mean density of the activated receptors across the brain* rather than with where those receptors sit. Our own composition-dominated finding (§3.2) is the same phenomenon at a different scale and in a different phylum, reached independently and with a different statistical instrument. That convergence is evidence that the finding is a property of receptor-weighted network perturbation, not an artefact of one implementation.

**Insect-versus-vertebrate receptor pharmacology** supplies the evidence base: neonicotinoid selectivity at nicotinic receptors [8], [9], [35]–[37] and partial agonism at mammalian α7 [26]; phenylpyrazole and cyclodiene action at GABA-gated chloride channels [16], [27], [29], [31] with the β3-homopentamer caveat that undercuts naive "vertebrate-safe" framing [32]; avermectin selectivity for glutamate-gated chloride channels [28], [33], [38]; sodium-channel modulation with differential *kdr* sensitivity [23], [67]; target-site resistance alleles [13]–[25]; single-cell expression atlases [39]–[44]; and combination-effect null models [45], [46], [48], [49] with empirical mixture data [47], [50], [51].

**Methodological neighbours.** The specification family of §2.4 is a multiverse analysis in the sense of Steegen *et al.* [74] and the specification curve of Simonsohn *et al.* [75], applied to a simulation rather than to a regression. The variance decomposition of §2.6 uses Saltelli cross-sampling [71] with Jansen estimators [72], validated on the Ishigami function [73], and the experiment ranking follows the classical value-of-information formulation [76].

**Position.** Table I states what is new narrowly. It is not "a connectome with drugs in it": that exists. It is the combination of typed pharmacological evidence, compound- and concentration-specific perturbation of *named, synapse-resolution* circuits, an insect/vertebrate comparison at one dose, and — the part we consider the transferable method — routine testing of whether each prediction needs the connectome at all.

**Table I. What each system provides.**

| Capability | Teaching sims [1]–[4] | Connectome LIF runtimes [7], [11] | FlyBrainLab [68] | Receptor-map whole-brain models [69], [70] | FlyLab |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Named cells at synapse resolution | no | yes | yes | no (regions) | yes |
| Compound **and free concentration** as the input | yes (organ level) | no | no | partly (receptor-map scaling) | yes |
| Typed evidence with per-row provenance | no | no | no | no | yes |
| Two species scored at the same dose | no | no | no | no | yes |
| Dependence of each prediction on the wiring, tested | no | rarely | no | partly [69] | yes, routinely |
| Specification robustness of the stated conclusions | no | no | no | no | yes |
| Whole analysis re-runs in a browser | some | no | no | no | yes |

Exposure modelling, mixtures and genotype shifts are implemented as supporting capabilities and are documented in the supplement (§S4); we do not present them as novelty claims.

We claim no new reconstruction, no fitted *in vivo* potency, no replacement for regulatory toxicology and no live-animal result. The `live_lab` field of every notebook this pipeline produced is `null`.

## 2. Methods

### 2.1 Typed pharmacological evidence (RQ1)

The v0.5 model, like most of this literature, applied a Hill function to whatever number a source reported and called the result *receptor occupancy*. That is wrong twice over. An EC50 or IC50 is a functional potency measured under particular assay conditions, so `C^n/(EC50^n + C^n)` is a normalised *functional response*, not the fraction of binding sites occupied; and a row with no sourced number at all must not yield a small response.

FlyLab v0.6 types the evidence instead. Each library row records the **parameter type** its source actually measured (`Kd`, `Ki`, `EC50`, `IC50`, `Kb`, `relative_potency`, `class_order`, `unknown`) and the **source relation**: how far that source sits from this compound, at this receptor, in this species (exact/exact/exact, exact receptor in another species, related receptor, class extrapolation, unsupported). Those two facts alone determine the **engagement model** the software may apply:

* `binding_occupancy` — legal only for a binding constant (`Kd`, `Ki`); here `C^n/(K^n + C^n)` *is* fractional occupancy;
* `functional_engagement` — the same algebra on a functional potency, reported as **engagement**, never as occupancy;
* `not_modelled` — no number is produced at all.

The refusal is enforced by the type system, not by convention: requesting an engagement from an unsupported or untyped row raises `EvidenceTypeError` rather than returning a value, and the library's own validator rejects a placeholder row that carries a number. Consequently a placeholder receptor returns **N/A** and is excluded from every numeric result and from the selectivity indices — the v0.5 behaviour, in which diazepam at insect RDL produced an engagement of 10⁻⁴ from a filler EC50 of 0.01 M, is now unrepresentable.

Throughout this paper we therefore say *engagement* where the underlying parameter is functional, and reserve *occupancy* for the rows that earn it.

### 2.2 The circuit substrate

Two committed cuts of MaleCNS v1.0 are used, both streamed out of the ~1.1 GB public weight matrix on a CI runner and each under about 1 MB of JSON: **`named`**, a 1-hop neighbourhood of `MN9` and `DNp01` ({{named_nodes}} nodes, {{named_edges}} edges), and **`taste_motor`**, a 1-hop neighbourhood of `MN9`, `DNp01` and the labellar gustatory types `LB1a`–`LB1d`, `LB3b`/`LB3c` plus an induced closure ({{taste_nodes}} nodes, {{taste_edges}} edges). A committed census of {{census_traced}} traced bodies with predicted transmitters supports whole-CNS summaries with no download. All {{lb_type_count}} `LB*` types named by the recent gustatory nomenclature are present in the MaleCNS annotations, giving {{sweet_grn_cells}} sweet and {{bitter_grn_cells}} bitter seed cells and {{paths_sweet}} sweet and {{paths_bitter}} bitter GRN→MN9 paths of at most {{paths_max_len}} synapses.

Two runtimes consume a gain patch: a deterministic clipped leaky rate iteration (~50 ms per evaluation of the `named` cut, which is what makes the permutation analyses affordable) and a current-based leaky integrate-and-fire network with the parameters of Shiu *et al.* [7]. The LIF arm's synaptic scale and its background Poisson drive are tuned constants, not fits; both are set out in §S3, because they set the LIF operating point and therefore every absolute LIF rate in this paper.

### 2.3 Connectome-dependence analysis (RQ2)

The instrument we consider most transferable asks, for one `compound × concentration × circuit × readout`, how much of the real connectome a prediction needs. The same drug contrast (treated minus vehicle, both arms on the *same* graph) is measured on progressively degraded copies of the cut, which form an **information ladder**:

| Graph model | What it still knows |
|---|---|
| `erdos_renyi` | node and edge counts, the weight histogram, the transmitter census |
| `rewire_degree_preserving` | + every node's in-degree, out-degree and transmitter |
| `weight_permute` | + the real edge list (weights reshuffled) |
| `sign_permute` | + the real weights on the real edges (only transmitter identity destroyed) |
| the MaleCNS cut | everything |

Each degradation preserves the node set, the node order and the seed block, so a shuffle can change the effect only through the wiring; definitions are in §S2. The statistic reported first is the **empirical two-sided permutation probability** `(k+1)/(n+1)`, with its resolution `1/(n+1)` printed beside it; `z` is secondary because a null distribution on a degraded graph is not normal. From the per-mode probabilities the analysis derives a **class** (topology-dependent, mixed, composition-dominated, no-effect, undefined) and the **necessary information level**: the weakest graph model on the ladder that already reproduces the effect. The class is a documented label, not a hypothesis test: no multiplicity correction is applied across the four modes and the threshold is a reporting convention.

Because the draws are i.i.d. given the seed, any prefix is itself a valid smaller permutation sample, so the permutation count is swept inside the same draws at no extra cost (§3.2).

### 2.4 The ablation ladder and the specification family (RQ3)

*Which layer carries the information?* Four models predict the same cell with one layer denied: **A** receptor engagement alone; **B** mechanism gains applied to the graph's transmitter composition only, with no edges; **C** the real cut and the real runtime with one generic, mechanism-free multiplier `g_all = max(0.05, 1 − θ_max)`; **D** the full model. The levels carry different units, so they are compared by how well each reproduces the full model's *ordering* of the compound library (Spearman ρ), never by value.

*Which conclusions are properties of the data rather than of a modelling choice?* The mapping from engagement to synaptic gain is the least defensible part of the model: the shipped rule for an insect nicotinic agonist, `g_ach = max(0.05, 1 + 0.4θ − 1.6θ²)`, encodes excitation followed by desensitisation and depolarisation block, which is a textbook picture but an asserted functional form. We therefore prespecify a finite family of {{stab_family_size}} admissible specifications — monotone-linear, saturating, weakly biphasic, the shipped biphasic rule and a strongly biphasic rule, each at five coefficient scales — and re-derive every principal conclusion of this paper under every member. The result is a **conclusion-stability matrix**: for each conclusion, the fraction of specifications that retain it and the names of those that do not. Undecidable cells (a readout that does not exist under that specification, for example a ratio whose denominator is silenced) count against the conclusion in the headline fraction and are reported separately. The fraction is a fraction of *that family*, not a posterior probability.

Two thresholds in the selectivity index are likewise conventions — the relative circuit change defining `C_circuit` (50 % by default) and the vertebrate engagement defining `C_vert` (20 %) — so the mechanism-level verdict is recomputed on the full 3×3 grid of {{thr_circuit_fracs}} × {{thr_vert_limits}}.

### 2.5 Evaluation taxonomy

Claims of the form "the model agrees with the literature" are worth very little unless the kind of agreement is named. We separate three:

1. **Implementation consistency** — the same inputs give the same outputs across transports, engines and runs: the browser-versus-native equality test, the determinism test, and the seeded regeneration of every figure and number (§3.4).
2. **Literature concordance** — the model's *ordering* of compounds agrees with published orderings. This is informative about coding errors and about the library, and it is scored with Spearman's ρ and Kendall's τ-b; but where the published ordering comes from a paper the library already cites for one of the compounds it orders, agreement is internal consistency, not validation. The pipeline computes that overlap from DOIs and PMIDs rather than asserting it.
3. **Independent, out-of-sample validation** — a comparison against data that entered neither the library nor the model. FlyLab currently has **none** of this at the circuit level, and says so.

### 2.6 Global uncertainty and the value of information

A one-at-a-time tornado cannot separate a factor's own effect from its interactions, so the model's uncertainty is attributed with a variance-based (Sobol') analysis over every assumption the implementation exposes: potency, Hill coefficient, the engagement-to-gain transformation and its coefficient, the external drive, the synapse-weight threshold of the cut, the transmitter-sign convention, the expression-weighting layer, and a deliberately null factor (the spiking RNG seed, which the deterministic rate engine ignores) whose index measures the estimator's noise floor at the sample size used. The design is Saltelli cross-sampling on two Latin-hypercube base matrices with Jansen estimators [71], [72]; the estimator is validated against the analytic Ishigami benchmark [73] in the same run.

The budget is then converted into a ranking of experiments by `VOI_j = S_j · Var(Y)` [76]: the model variance that would disappear if assumption *j* were resolved exactly while everything else stayed as uncertain as it is. The first-order index is the right one here — resolving one factor leaves its interactions with everything still unknown — and the total-order version is reported alongside as the upper bound obtained by resolving that factor last. This is variance of a model output under assumed input ranges, and is not an expected gain in accuracy about a living fly.

## 3. Results

Everything in this section is a property of the software, the typed library and one or two hops-limited MaleCNS cuts. All values are regenerated by `scripts/reproduce_paper.py` and stored keyed in `papers/results.json`.

### 3.1 RQ1: what the library actually rests on (Fig. F11, Table T10)

The typed census is the answer to RQ1 and, we think, the most useful artefact for anyone building a comparable model. Of {{ev_rows}} rows over {{ev_compounds}} compounds and {{ev_receptor_keys}} receptor keys, {{ev_param_EC50}} carry an EC50, {{ev_param_IC50}} an IC50 and exactly **{{ev_param_Kd}}** a genuine binding constant; {{ev_rows_not_modelled}} carry no modellable evidence and return N/A. By source relation, {{ev_rel_exact_compound_exact_receptor_exact_species}} rows are this compound at this receptor in this species, {{ev_rel_exact_compound_exact_receptor_other_species}} are the right receptor in another species, {{ev_rel_exact_compound_related_receptor}} a related receptor, {{ev_rel_class_extrapolation}} a class extrapolation and {{ev_rel_unsupported}} unsupported. The single row entitled to a physical binding-occupancy model is {{ev_binding_row_names}}; every other engagement in this paper is a normalised functional response.

Three consequences are worth stating because they change results rather than wording. First, the placeholder rows no longer contribute: diazepam at insect RDL now reports “{{dia_insect_rdl_occ}}” instead of the small number the v0.5 model derived from a filler potency of 0.01 M, and it is excluded from the numeric selectivity block rather than dragging it. Second, the nicotinic key has been split by subunit, because it was one key where the biology has several receptors: spinosad is now scored at α6 and imidacloprid at β1, while the remaining nicotinic compounds stay on a documented aggregate key precisely because their sources used hybrid constructs and cannot support a subunit claim. Third, checking each source against what it reports corrected one of our own descriptions: the neonicotinoid potency row we had attributed to a *Drosophila* receptor is measured on a cat-flea α1/α2 plus chicken β2 hybrid, which is now recorded in the row and reflected in its relation.

At 1 µM, imidacloprid engages the insect nicotinic row at {{imi_insect_occ}} against {{imi_vert_occ}} at vertebrate α4β2 ({{imi_ec50_ratio}}-fold potency ratio, {{window_log10_imidacloprid}} log₁₀ units of window), and fipronil engages insect RDL at {{fip_insect_occ}} against {{fip_vert_occ}} at native heteromeric vertebrate GABA-A ({{fip_ec50_ratio}}-fold). The second contrast is the honest one: that vertebrate engagement is substantial, and it follows from a library correction (a row that contradicted its own citation by roughly nine-fold) that removed a "vertebrate-safe" claim the v0.4 draft could have made in passing.

### 3.2 RQ2: which predictions need the connectome (Figs. F6, F12; Tables T6, T11, T12)

At {{dep_n}} permutations per mode (resolution {{dep_p_floor}}), imidacloprid's effect on the `named` cut's mean rate is {{dep_named_imidacloprid_effect}} Hz and is **{{dep_named_imidacloprid_class}}**: every structure-preserving degradation reproduces it — sign-permutation *p* = {{dep_named_imidacloprid_p_sign_permute}}, weight-permutation *p* = {{dep_named_imidacloprid_p_weight_permute}}, degree-preserving rewiring *p* = {{dep_named_imidacloprid_p_rewire_degree_preserving}} — while only the Erdős–Rényi control is beaten at the resolution floor ({{dep_named_imidacloprid_p_erdos_renyi}}). Its necessary information level is `{{dep_named_imidacloprid_level}}`: a graph that knows only every node's degree and transmitter already reproduces the drug effect. Because `sign_permute` preserves the transmitter histogram exactly, the interpretation is specific: an agonist that collapses cholinergic gain removes a fixed share of excitatory weight wherever that weight sits.

Fipronil is the contrast. Its effect of {{dep_named_fipronil_effect}} Hz is **{{dep_named_fipronil_class}}**, with weight-permutation *p* = {{dep_named_fipronil_p_weight_permute}} and degree-preserving rewiring *p* = {{dep_named_fipronil_p_rewire_degree_preserving}} against a non-significant sign-permutation *p* = {{dep_named_fipronil_p_sign_permute}}; the necessary level is `{{dep_named_fipronil_level}}`. Disinhibition depends on *where* the inhibition is, and that is a wiring fact.

The same test applied to the map-extracted taste-motor arm gives a **properly powered negative**. At {{dep_n_taste}} permutations, the fipronil bitter-veto ratio is not distinguishable from any of its four shuffled controls (*p* = {{dep_taste_fipronil_p_sign_permute}}, {{dep_taste_fipronil_p_weight_permute}}, {{dep_taste_fipronil_p_rewire_degree_preserving}}, {{dep_taste_fipronil_p_erdos_renyi}}; max |z| = {{dep_taste_fipronil_max_abs_z}}), so the veto ratio is model behaviour rather than evidence about the wiring. Imidacloprid has no veto ratio at all on this path: at 1 µM it silences MN9, so the ratio is *undefined*, not zero — a direct consequence of the agonist rule reaching its 0.05 floor.

Over the whole library ({{dep_land_compounds}} compounds × {{dep_land_concs}} concentrations = {{dep_land_cells}} cells at {{dep_n_landscape}} permutations), **{{dep_land_topology_dependent}}** cells are topology-dependent, **{{dep_land_composition_dominated}}** composition-dominated, {{dep_land_no_effect}} show no effect and {{dep_land_mixed}} are mixed. The topology-dependent set is chemically coherent: it is exactly the chloride-channel blockers ({{dep_land_topology_compounds}}). This is the result we would want another group to try to break: it says that on this readout the connectome earns its place for compounds that remove inhibition, and does not for compounds that scale excitation.

The permutation-count sweep separates two questions that are usually conflated. A *verdict* at α = 0.05 settles early: it stops moving from *n* = {{dep_verdict_stable_from_n}} shuffles on the easiest arm and from *n* = {{dep_verdict_stable_to_n}} on the hardest. A quotable mid-range *p* is dearer: reaching ±0.02 of its final value took between {{dep_p_within_0p02_from_n}} and {{dep_p_within_0p02_worst_n}} permutations depending on the arm. A screening landscape and a headline claim are therefore not the same computation, and the software reports which one it just did.

### 3.3 RQ2 continued: which layer carries the information (Fig. F13, Table T13)

Ablation over {{abl_compounds}} compounds is blunt. Receptor engagement alone orders the library **backwards** (ρ {{abl_rho_receptor_min}} to {{abl_rho_receptor_max}} across concentrations). The composition-only baseline — mechanism gains applied to the cut's transmitter proportions, with no edges at all — **reproduces the full model's ordering** (ρ {{abl_rho_composition_min}} to {{abl_rho_composition_max}}; ρ {{abl_rho_composition_nicotinic}} within the {{abl_nicotinic_n}} nicotinic agonists, where ordering is hardest). The real cut with a generic, mechanism-free multiplier is the **worst** level of the four (ρ {{abl_rho_topology_paper}}, {{abl_rms_topology_hz}} Hz rms residual against the full model).

Read together with §3.2 the message is unambiguous and slightly deflating for connectome pharmacology: nearly all of the ordering information lives in the mechanism rules and the composition they act on, and the connectome without the pharmacology is not a cheap substitute for the connectome with it. What the connectome adds is not a better ordering but the identification of the compounds whose effects depend on wiring — which is a different and, for this method, more useful product.

### 3.4 RQ3: which conclusions survive (Fig. F14, Tables T14, T15)

Under the {{stab_family_size}} prespecified specifications:

* "A nicotinic agonist suppresses circuit activity" is retained by only {{stab_C1_retained}} of {{stab_family_size}} and is **reversed** by all {{stab_C1_lost}} monotone specifications — every linear and saturating rule at every coefficient scale. Under the default biphasic rule, imidacloprid at 1 µM drives `g_ach` to {{stab_default_g_ach}} and the network from {{stab_default_vehicle_hz}} Hz to {{stab_default_treated_hz}} Hz; under a monotone rule the *same compound at the same engagement* takes `g_ach` to {{stab_monotone_g_ach}} and **excites** the network, from {{stab_monotone_vehicle_hz}} Hz to {{stab_monotone_treated_hz}} Hz ({{stab_monotone_pct_change}}). Suppression is therefore a property of the −1.6θ² desensitisation term, not of the pharmacology–connectome integration, and no reader should carry it away as the latter.
* "The neighbourhood buffers nicotinic selectivity" is retained {{stab_C5_retained}} of {{stab_family_size}} and **reversed by none**; its {{stab_C5_undecidable}} failures are undecidable, because under those specifications the circuit never crosses the threshold that defines the index. The monotone rules that reverse the suppression claim buffer *more*, not less.
* "The neighbourhood amplifies Nav/AChE selectivity" is the weakest amplification claim at {{stab_C6_retained}} of {{stab_family_size}}.
* The two topology conclusions, the RDL disinhibition and the map bitter-veto direction are retained by **every** specification ({{stab_specification_independent}}). The RQ2 results of §3.2 are thus specification-independent, which is what makes them quotable.

The threshold grid tells the same story about conventions. Overall the amplify/buffer split is **not** stable (`stable = {{thr_stable}}`): nicotinic buffering holds in {{thr_nicotinic_matching}} of {{thr_nicotinic_cells}} cells with a mean gap ranging from {{thr_nicotinic_gap_max}} to {{thr_nicotinic_gap_min}} log₁₀ units, while Nav/AChE amplification holds in {{thr_nav_ache_matching}} of {{thr_nav_ache_cells}} and flips to buffering at the strictest corner ({{thr_nav_ache_failing}}, gap {{thr_nav_ache_gap_min}}). We therefore quote ranges over the grid, never the point estimate; the corresponding v0.5 sentence quoted a point estimate and should not have.

### 3.5 RQ3 continued: where the uncertainty lives, and what to measure next (Fig. F15, Tables T16, T17)

The Sobol' analysis ({{unc_evaluations}} model evaluations at n_base = {{unc_n_base}}, Var(Y) = {{unc_variance}} Hz²) attributes the variance of the neighbourhood mean rate to two assumptions and no others: the **engagement-to-gain transformation** (S1 {{unc_S_gain_transform}}, ST {{unc_ST_gain_transform}}) and the cut's **synapse-weight threshold** (S1 {{unc_S_weight_threshold}}, ST {{unc_ST_weight_threshold}}), with the external drive a distant third ({{unc_S_drive}}) and everything else at the estimator's noise floor of ±{{unc_noise_floor}} — a floor measured, not assumed, by the null factor. Potency, Hill coefficient and the gain coefficient are indistinguishable from zero at a saturating dose ({{unc_at_noise_floor}}), which is the quantitative form of a result the v0.5 tornado already showed: a single high dose carries no information about a potency parameter. Interactions carry {{unc_interaction_share}} of the variance, so no single experiment removes much on its own. On the analytic benchmark the same estimator returns {{ish_estimated}} against the exact {{ish_analytic}} at n_base = {{ish_n_base}} (max error {{ish_max_abs_error}}), so the attribution above is not an artefact of the estimator.

Converted into experiments, the ranking is actionable and mildly uncomfortable. The top factor is **{{voi_rank1_factor}}** ({{voi_rank1_var}} of {{voi_var_total}} Hz²), resolved by *{{voi_rank1_experiment}}* — recording postsynaptic responses at a known synapse across a concentration ladder and reading the *shape* of the response-versus-engagement curve, which FlyLab currently asserts. That is precisely the assumption §3.4 shows the suppression conclusion turns on. The second, **{{voi_rank2_factor}}** ({{voi_rank2_var}} Hz²), needs **no experiment at all**: it is resolved by re-running the analysis against synapse-confidence strata of data already held. An honest ranking of what to do next should be allowed to put "analyse what you have" above "go to the bench", and this one does.

### 3.6 Supporting behaviour and literature concordance (Figs. F5, F7, F10; Tables T4, T5, T8)

Driving the {{veto_n_sweet_grn}} real sweet GRN body IDs at {{veto_sugar_hz}} Hz and reading MN9 gives a complete bitter veto in vehicle on both engines (ratio {{veto_rate_vehicle_ratio}} rate, {{veto_lif_vehicle_ratio}} LIF), partially relieved by fipronil ({{veto_rate_fipronil_ratio}} rate, {{veto_lif_fipronil_ratio}} LIF), matching the published direction [7], [63]. The two engines agree on **direction only**: sugar-driven MN9 is {{veto_rate_vehicle_sugar}} Hz on the rate engine against {{veto_lif_vehicle_sugar}} Hz on the LIF engine, a difference of more than two orders of magnitude that follows from the LIF background drive (§S3). This is not cross-model validation and we do not present it as such.

The selectivity landscape splits by mechanism rather than potency — Nav/AChE compounds amplified ({{landscape_amplify_compounds}}), nicotinic compounds buffered ({{landscape_buffer_compounds}}) — subject to the threshold caveat of §3.4. Compounds that never reach the circuit threshold on either cut ({{landscape_unscored_compounds}}) receive no circuit index and are reported as unscored rather than as buffered.

Literature concordance over published orderings gives mean Spearman ρ = {{rank_mean_rho}} across {{rank_evaluated}} evaluable entry–assay combinations, with {{rank_skipped}} skipped for want of library coverage and {{rank_known_discrepancies}} inversions recorded rather than repaired ({{rank_discrepancy_ids}}); both are receptor-parameter versus whole-animal-potency differences, and editing a library row to improve ρ would destroy the provenance chain for a cosmetic gain. Of the {{rank_entries_evaluable}} distinct published orderings the library can cover, {{rank_shared_source}} come from a publication the library already cites for a compound in that ordering and are therefore *not* out-of-sample, while {{rank_source_disjoint}} are source-disjoint. None is an independent test of the *circuit* model, because all of them score the ordering of receptor-level quantities; the circuit layer has no out-of-sample test at all.

The mixture arm is a **limited qualitative concordance check**, not validation: {{mix_n_matching}} of {{mix_n_targets}} published binary-mixture verdicts are reproduced, but the reference organism is {{mix_species}} rather than *Drosophila*, {{mix_n_substituted}} of the partners are absent from the library and represented by same-class substitutes, and the endpoint is a three-valued verdict. It is evidence that the Bliss/Loewe selection rule behaves sensibly, and nothing more.

### 3.7 RQ4: reproduction (Fig. F1)

One command regenerates every figure, table and number in this paper from committed artifacts, with no download and no access to the 1.1 GB weight matrix:

```bash
python -m pip install -e ".[dev,viz]"
python scripts/reproduce_paper.py          # full run
python scripts/reproduce_paper.py --fast   # reduced permutation and replicate counts
python scripts/reproduce_paper.py --only dependence --outdir /tmp/x
```

Every stochastic component — library jitter, drive jitter, the LIF Poisson drive, graph shuffles, bootstrap resampling, the Saltelli sample — is seeded from one `--seed`, so two runs at the same commit produce identical values; `--only` is incremental and cannot silently truncate the record; and `--jobs` changes the wall clock only. The output is `papers/figures/*.png` at 300 dpi with generated captions, `papers/tables/T*.csv`/`.md`, and `papers/results.json`: every scalar quoted above with its value, rendered text, unit and producing step, plus a provenance block carrying the version, git SHA, library SHA-256, map identifiers, graph sizes and seeds. This manuscript and its supplement are rendered from templates by substituting those keys, so a number cannot drift between code and prose; `tests/test_reproduce.py` asserts that every key either document references exists in the record, and that the record did not come from a `--fast` run.

The same wheel runs in a browser tab on Pyodide and is published as a static site, so the assays behind these figures can be re-run by opening one link with nothing installed; `tests/test_browser_bridge.py` asserts that the browser and server transports return equal JSON — every readout, warning and provenance field — for a representative call to each route. Two honest asymmetries: `git_sha` is `null` in a browser notebook because there is no git in WebAssembly (the build SHA lives in the site manifest instead), and the browser is slower by roughly an order of magnitude on the spiking assay. The interactive dashboard exposes the dependence, stability and uncertainty results directly; see the dashboard panel if present in the version you are running.

Data licences: MaleCNS v1.0 and FlyWire are CC-BY 4.0 from their consortia [5], [6] and must be cited before this software; the derived cuts inherit that licence and record it. Literature datasets carry a DOI or PMID per value. FlyLab's own code is MIT. An archival Zenodo deposit of the tagged release, the derived cuts and `results.json` will carry the DOI **[to be minted on acceptance]**.

## 4. Discussion

**What was learned computationally.** Three things, none of which is a biological claim. First, typing the evidence changes results, not just documentation: refusing to model unsupported rows removed a class of small, plausible, entirely invented responses that the previous version reported and that no reader could have distinguished from measurements. Second, the dependence analysis shows that *most* of what a receptor-perturbation model on a connectome predicts is carried by the composition of the network rather than its topology — and that the exceptions are chemically coherent, which is what makes the distinction worth computing per prediction rather than arguing in general. Third, specification robustness relocated the paper's most quotable result: the direction of a nicotinic agonist's circuit effect is set by an asserted functional form, and the variance analysis independently nominates that same assumption as the dominant one and as the experiment to do next. Two different instruments converging on the same weak point is the strongest methodological result here.

**Model-generated observations versus encoded assumptions.** It is worth separating these explicitly, because a simulation paper that does not can appear to discover its own inputs. *Encoded*: the insect/vertebrate potency contrasts (they are library values from the cited literature); the direction of each mechanism's action; the biphasic shape of the nicotinic rule; the sign convention that makes glutamate globally inhibitory; the transmitter predictions, which are themselves model output from another group [10], [12]. *Model-generated*: which compounds' effects survive which graph degradation; the classification of the library into topology-dependent and composition-dominated; the ordering information carried by each ablation level; the fraction of specifications retaining each conclusion; the variance attribution and the experiment ranking; the observation that the circuit threshold is never reached for the compounds listed in §3.6; and the undefined veto ratio under a saturating agonist. The bitter veto itself is in between: its *direction* is a published result [7], [63] reproduced here as a directional control, not a finding. The separation is itself generated rather than asserted: an audit of the dependency chain behind a circuit readout returns {{claims_chain_links}} links (Table T18), of which exactly {{claims_observed}} — the MaleCNS edges — is a measurement of the system being simulated, {{claims_literature_derived}} reuses a measurement of something else, {{claims_model_assumption}} are asserted modelling choices and {{claims_computed}} are arithmetic on the links above them; the companion split reports {{claims_n_facts}} facts, {{claims_n_inference}} model inferences and {{claims_n_unknown}} unknowns for the same result. A reader who wants the provenance of a number does not have to reconstruct it from this prose.

**Relation to adjacent work.** The closest published parallel is the receptor-map perturbation of human whole-brain models [69], [70], where the effect of a simulated intervention tracked mean receptor density rather than its regional placement [69]. We reach a structurally identical conclusion in an insect connectome at synapse resolution, with an explicit permutation test rather than a correlation, and we can further say *which* compounds escape it. Relative to connectome runtimes [7], [11] and to executable-circuit platforms [68], the difference is not that FlyLab simulates circuits — they do that, and better at scale — but that a compound and a concentration enter through typed evidence, and that each prediction is accompanied by a statement of what it depends on. Relative to teaching simulators [1]–[4], FlyLab is not a replacement: it has no organ physiology and no behavioural endpoint.

**Intended use.** As an instrument for *hypothesis triage*: deciding which compound, concentration and readout combinations are worth an electrophysiological or behavioural experiment, and which are predictable from network composition alone and therefore uninformative about wiring. As a provenance-preserving compilation of scattered pharmacological evidence, with the gaps visible. As a teaching object in which the distance between a published EC50 and a circuit prediction is made explicit at every step. And as a reproducible template for the three tests we consider transferable — dependence, ablation and specification robustness — which require no fly and no connectome in particular.

**What the software cannot support.** It cannot predict a dose–response in an animal: there is no *Drosophila* pharmacokinetics to speak of, so a free concentration at the receptor is an *input*, never an output. It cannot support a claim of insect-versus-vertebrate safety: the vertebrate side is a receptor panel with no circuit, and one library correction has already shown how fragile such a claim is. It cannot support a quantitative subunit-level selectivity claim for the nicotinic compounds whose sources used hybrid constructs. It cannot support any claim that the specific MaleCNS wiring matters for a compound the landscape classes as composition-dominated. It cannot support absolute firing rates: the rate and LIF engines differ by more than two orders of magnitude on the same contrast, and only their direction agrees. And it cannot support anything about a living animal at all: there are no live data in this repository, and no code path can write any.

**Model-specific caveats.** Four deserve discussion rather than a list. (i) *Glutamate is signed inhibitory globally.* This follows the insect GluCl convention and is right for many *Drosophila* synapses, but it is a per-transmitter assumption applied per cell, so any cell with excitatory glutamatergic output is mis-signed; the sign-permutation null exists partly to bound how much that matters, and for the composition-dominated majority it matters less than one would fear, because the effect does not depend on which cell carries which transmitter. (ii) *Receptor effects are applied across transmitter-defined synapses.* A gain on `g_ach` scales every cholinergic synapse, whether or not that synapse expresses the receptor the compound binds. This is the assumption the expression layer relaxes, and it is why that layer is reported as a bound rather than as an improvement: coverage is only {{expr_coverage_overall}} of cells, and receptor expression in identified adult motor neurons — MN9's class — is a confirmed gap in the atlas literature, so the paper's principal readout carries the default weight. Weighting shifts MN9 by {{expr_mn9_delta_hz}} Hz at 1 µM imidacloprid; we report that as the size of the uncertainty, not as a correction. (iii) *Hops-limited cuts.* A 1-hop neighbourhood with a weight floor is a fraction of MN9's real input; missing inhibition can flip the sign of any readout, and the disinhibition readout is bounded by the GABA share that survived the cut. Growing the cut is deliberately constrained: past about 1 MB of JSON the weight floor is raised rather than hops added, because a 2-hop cut of a motor neuron in a 25-million-edge CNS is most of the animal. (iv) *The rate-versus-LIF discrepancy* (§3.6) is agreement of direction only.

**Prospective, not pre-registered.** The seven hypotheses in §S5 are *prospective*: written before any experiment, labelled `software_prediction`, with `live_result` null. The v0.5 draft called them "pre-registered", which they are not — a mutable GitHub repository is not a registry. They will become pre-registered only if the tagged release is externally archived with a timestamp, and the wording will change at that point and not before. The suggested sample sizes that accompanied them are an **illustrative planning minimum** computed after capping a standardised effect at d = 1.0, because model-internal intervals contain no biological variance and produce absurd effect sizes; they are kept in the supplement and should not be used to power a study.

## 5. Conclusion

FlyLab couples typed pharmacological evidence to compound- and concentration-specific perturbation of named, synapse-resolution *Drosophila* circuits, and ships three tests that the coupling makes possible: connectome-dependence analysis, an ablation ladder and a conclusion-stability matrix, with a variance-based uncertainty budget that turns into a ranked experiment list. Applied to its own results, the method establishes that a minority of predictions — the chloride-channel blockers — need the specific wiring, that most do not, that the ordering information lives in the mechanism rules rather than the topology, and that the paper's most quotable circuit result is a property of an asserted functional form rather than of the integration. Those are uncomfortable findings for the tool, which is the point: they were produced by instruments built into it, and they are reproducible from committed artifacts by one command, natively or in a browser.

What would change the picture is short and specific, and the uncertainty budget rather than taste now says what it is: a calibration of receptor engagement against synaptic gain at a known synapse, which would convert the dominant modelling choice into a measurement; a reconstruction-confidence analysis, which needs no bench time at all; and one live *Drosophila* assay, which would move the prospective predictions out of the supplement. Until then this is a description of a method and of a tool that tests itself.

## Statements

**AI assistance.** The author defined the research questions, the methods and the analyses; verified the source literature, including checking each cited publication against the value attributed to it; executed and reviewed every analysis; and accepts full responsibility for the scientific content of this article. AI assistance was used for software implementation (code, tests, figures and pipeline scaffolding) and for drafting and editing prose. No data were generated, imputed, estimated or extrapolated by a language model; every number in the results, tables and figures was produced by the reproduction pipeline from committed artifacts and is keyed in `papers/results.json`.

**Animal research.** No animal experiments were performed and no live-animal data are reported. The `live_lab` field of every notebook produced by this pipeline is `null`. The protocols in `protocols/` are unexecuted drafts, included so that a power calculation can be done before any animal is used.

**Data and code availability.** Code: https://github.com/pinkysworld/FlyLab (MIT). Connectome data: official MaleCNS v1.0 and FlyWire releases, CC-BY 4.0, downloaded and not redistributed; derived cuts are committed under `data/derived/` with their citation strings. Literature datasets: `data/literature/`, each value carrying a DOI or PMID.

**Competing interests.** None declared. **Funding.** None.

